1 FORTRAN
    The FORTRAN command compiles a VAX FORTRAN source file into a VAX
    MACRO-32 source file (.MAR).  MACRO assembles that file into an
    object module, and LINK links the object into an image RUN runs:

        FORTRAN SQUARES         ! SQUARES.FOR -> SQUARES.MAR
        MACRO SQUARES           ! SQUARES.MAR -> SQUARES.OBJ
        LINK SQUARES            ! SQUARES.OBJ -> SQUARES.EXE
        RUN SQUARES

    The MACRO file is meant to be read: it holds the FORTRAN source as
    comments, each line with its number, so the code and the FORTRAN it
    came from are side by side.  A comment names each INCLUDE file, whose
    lines appear only with INCLUDE's /LIST, with their include depth
    before their numbers.

    The compiler takes VAX FORTRAN V5.0, FORTRAN-77 with DEC's extensions:
    main programs, subroutines, functions
    of every type, ENTRY, and BLOCK DATA; declarations of every type,
    IMPLICIT, PARAMETER, arrays, COMMON, EQUIVALENCE, DATA, SAVE,
    EXTERNAL, and INTRINSIC; DEC's STRUCTURE, RECORD, UNION, and MAP,
    octal, hexadecimal, Hollerith, and RADIX-50 constants, VOLATILE,
    AUTOMATIC, and STATIC, and the CDEC$ directives (IDENT, PSECT,
    TITLE, SUBTITLE, OPTIONS, and END OPTIONS); assignment and
    expressions in every type and length (BYTE, LOGICAL, INTEGER, REAL
    in F, D or G, and H, COMPLEX, and CHARACTER, with substrings and
    concatenation), and every intrinsic function, in line or by MTHRTL;
    every IF, DO, and GO TO, CALL, RETURN, STOP, and PAUSE; READ, WRITE,
    and the other I/O statements, and OPEN and the other file statements,
    on sequential, relative, and indexed files, the keyed READ with every
    key specifier among them.  DICTIONARY draws %FORT-E-NOTIMPL.

    From a later DEC FORTRAN, a FUNCTION or SUBROUTINE may be RECURSIVE
    (RECURSIVE INTEGER FUNCTION FACT(N) RESULT(F)), as may every one in
    a file under /RECURSIVE or after OPTIONS /RECURSIVE.  Such a routine
    may call itself, and each activation has its own variables, but for
    those SAVE'd, given initial values, in COMMON, or STATIC, and its own
    temporaries, so an AST or a handler may also enter it while it runs.
    In the routine, its name with an argument list is a call.  Without
    RESULT the name alone is the function's value; RESULT(name) names
    another variable for it instead.  AUTOMATIC alone doesn't make a routine
    safe to enter twice: its temporaries stay static.

    A program compiles, links, and runs:

        FORTRAN AVERAGE
        MACRO AVERAGE
        LINK AVERAGE
        RUN AVERAGE
        Mean:   3.750000

    The compiler checks the program units of one file against each
    other: a function's type where it is used, and the number of
    arguments of each call.

    A program may be several files, each compiled and assembled on its
    own, then linked together.  A common block of one name is the same
    storage in each unit that declares it, as long as the longest
    declaration.  A BLOCK DATA may give it initial values from another
    file; name the BLOCK DATA in an EXTERNAL statement, so that LINK
    reports it if it's missing and finds it in a library:

        FORTRAN STORE,SHOW,START
        MACRO STORE
        MACRO SHOW
        MACRO START
        LINK STORE,SHOW,START

    INCLUDE 'file' reads a file, by default of type .FOR, found relative
    to the file that includes it: on the host, in its directory; on a
    volume, on its device and in its directory.  INCLUDE 'file/LIST' puts
    the file's lines in the MACRO file; /NOLIST is the default.

    INCLUDE '(module)' reads a text library's module (see HELP LIBRARY),
    from the first of these that has it: the libraries given with the
    source (FORTRAN PROG+MYLIB/LIBRARY), the library the logical name
    FORT$LIBRARY names, SYS$LIBRARY:FORSYSDEF.TLB, and last the definition
    modules built into govax, such as $SSDEF, $IODEF, $JPIDEF, $FORDEF,
    $CHFDEF, and $SYSSRVNAM, whose INTEGER*4 and PARAMETER statements give
    VMS's names their values.  INCLUDE 'lib(module)' reads only that library.

    A program can use VMS directly: a system service or RTL routine is a
    routine like any other, called as a function for its status, with
    arguments left out (STATUS = SYS$GETJPIW(,,,LIST,IOSB,,)), %VAL, %REF,
    %DESCR, and %LOC.  A FORTRAN function can be a condition handler
    (LIB$ESTABLISH), and a subroutine an AST routine or exit handler.
    The system subroutines are there: DATE, TIME, IDATE, EXIT, SECNDS,
    RAN, MVBITS, and ERRSNS.

    Compiled programs call FTNRTL, govax's FORTRAN run-time library, a
    shareable image written in MACRO-32 and built into govax.  LINK
    finds its routines (named FTN$...) without an options file, and RUN
    finds the image.  Both look for a file of one's own first: a logical
    name FTNRTL, SYS$SHARE:FTNRTL.EXE, or FTNRTL.EXE in the host library
    directory.  FTNRTL has FTN$VERSION, FTN$STOP, the I/O
    statements' calls (FTN$READ_SF, FTN$WRITE_SF, and the other begin
    calls, FTN$IO_ITEM, FTN$IO_END), ISHFTC (FTN$IISHFTC,
    FTN$JISHFTC), and a routine for each intrinsic function MTHRTL
    lacks, for a program that passes the function's name as an argument
    (FTN$LEN, FTN$INDEX, ..., FTN$NWORKERS), which MACRO programs can
    call too.  List-directed output goes to SYS$OUTPUT in records of 80
    bytes, each starting with a blank (its carriage control), each value
    in its type's format: LOGICAL as T or F but for LOGICAL*1 and BYTE,
    an integer in 5 columns, INTEGER*2 in 7 columns, INTEGER*4 in 12, REAL
    with 7 significant digits, REAL*8 with 16 (15 with /G_FLOATING),
    REAL*16 with 33, COMPLEX as its two parts in parentheses, as
    (1.000000,-2.500000), and CHARACTER as its text.

    Syntax:

        FORTRAN <source>[+<source>...][+<library>/LIBRARY][,...][/HOST]
                [/MACRO[=<file>] | /NOMACRO]
                [/[NO]EXTEND_SOURCE] [/[NO]D_LINES]
                [/CONTINUATIONS=<n>] [/[NO]I4] [/[NO]G_FLOATING]
                [/ALIGN[=<value>] | /NOALIGN] [/[NO]OPTIMIZE]
                [/LIST[=<file>] | /NOLIST]
                [/SHOW[=(<keyword>,...)] | /NOSHOW]
                [/[NO]CROSS_REFERENCE]

    Parameter:

        <source>        The source file, or several separated by commas,
                        each compiled to its own MACRO file.  Files joined
                        by + are one compilation, to one MACRO file named
                        for the first, and a file followed by /LIBRARY is
                        a text library (.TLB by default) for that
                        compilation's INCLUDE statements.  Without a
                        file type a source is .FOR.  A source can be a
                        host file or a file on a mounted volume, by the
                        rules MACRO's source follows (HELP MACRO): /HOST
                        makes each a host path, a name with a directory
                        in brackets or a device is on a volume, a name
                        with "/" is a host path, and a bare name follows
                        SET DEFAULT.  Quote a host path to keep its case.

    Qualifiers:

        /MACRO[=<file>] Writes the MACRO file.  This is the default.
                        Without a file name, it has the source's name and
                        location, with the type MAR in the same case as
                        the source's type: squares.for gives squares.mar.
                        A name is placed as MACRO's /OBJECT= places its
                        object, and a VMS name without a type gets MAR.
                        On a volume, the file is the next version of its
                        name.  A host source whose type is already MAR is
                        refused unless /MACRO= names another file, since
                        its MACRO file would replace it.

        /NOMACRO        Compiles the source and reports any errors, but
                        writes no MACRO file.

        /EXTEND_SOURCE  Lets the statement field of a fixed-format line
        /NOEXTEND_SOURCE
                        run to column 132 instead of column 72.  The
                        default is /NOEXTEND_SOURCE.  An OPTIONS statement
                        overrides this for the unit after it.

        /D_LINES        Compiles the debugging lines (those with a D in
        /NOD_LINES      column 1) as statements.  The default is
                        /NOD_LINES: they are comments.

        /CONTINUATIONS=<n>
                        The most continuation lines one statement may
                        have, from 0 to 99.  The default is 19.

        /I4             INTEGER and LOGICAL with no length, and implied
        /NOI4           integers, are 4 bytes.  With /NOI4 they are 2, and
                        an integer constant from -32768 to 32767 is an
                        INTEGER*2.  The default is /I4.  A unit's OPTIONS
                        statement overrides this for that unit.

        /G_FLOATING     REAL*8 (DOUBLE PRECISION) values are G_floating:
        /NOG_FLOATING   53 bits of precision, about 15 decimal digits, and
                        a range of about 0.56E-308 to 0.9E308.  With
                        /NOG_FLOATING they are D_floating: 56 bits, about
                        16 digits, and REAL's range, about 0.29E-38 to
                        1.7E38.  The default is /NOG_FLOATING.  A unit's
                        OPTIONS statement overrides this for that unit;
                        the units of a program must agree, since a value
                        passed from one format to the other is misread.

        /ALIGN[=<value>] How structures and common blocks are laid out.
        /NOALIGN        /NOALIGN, the default, packs them, as VAX FORTRAN
                        V5.0 does.  /ALIGN puts each field and common name
                        on its natural boundary.  A value is ALL, NONE,
                        NATURAL, PACKED, or <class>=<rule>, and several go
                        in parentheses: the classes are RECORDS (or
                        STRUCTURES) and COMMONS, and the rules PACKED,
                        NATURAL, and for COMMONS STANDARD, at most a
                        longword.  /ALIGN alone is ALL.  CDEC$ OPTIONS
                        groups in the source override it.

        /OPTIMIZE       Runs the optimizer's passes, for code that does what
        /NOOPTIMIZE     the program says in fewer instructions.  The passes
                        run are named in the MACRO file's header.  The
                        default is /OPTIMIZE.

        /LIST[=<file>]  Writes a listing: the source's lines with their
        /NOLIST         numbers, each message under its line, and each
                        unit's storage map.  By default it is the source's
                        name with the type LIS, beside the source, and it is
                        written after errors too.  The default is /NOLIST.
                        CDEC$ TITLE and SUBTITLE set the heading of the page
                        after the one they are on; with no TITLE, a page's
                        title is its unit's name.

        /SHOW[=(<keyword>,...)]  What a listing holds: ALL, NONE, [NO]INCLUDE
        /NOSHOW         (every INCLUDE file's lines, whatever INCLUDE's own
                        /LIST says), and [NO]MAP.  /SHOW alone is ALL and
                        /NOSHOW NONE; with no /SHOW, a listing has the maps,
                        and only the INCLUDE files that say /LIST.

        /CROSS_REFERENCE  A listing follows each unit's map with where its
        /NOCROSS_REFERENCE  names and labels are mentioned: a line number each,
                        with D if the line defines the name and A if it
                        assigns it; 1:3 is line 3 of a file included at
                        depth 1.  It is there after errors too.  Without
                        /LIST it does nothing but say so.  The default is
                        /NOCROSS_REFERENCE.

        /CHECK[=(<keyword>,...)]  What the compiled code looks out for: ALL,
        /NOCHECK        NONE, [NO]BOUNDS (subscripts and substrings: the
                        subscript range trap; an adjustable array's bounds:
                        FOR$_ADJARRDIM), [NO]OVERFLOW (integer overflow traps),
                        and [NO]UNDERFLOW (floating underflow faults).  /CHECK
                        alone is ALL.  The default is /NOCHECK.  An OPTIONS
                        statement before a unit may change it for that unit.

        /WARNINGS[=(<keyword>,...)]  What the compiler says, beyond errors: ALL,
        /NOWARNINGS     NONE, [NO]GENERAL (its warnings and informational
                        messages; on by default), [NO]DECLARATIONS (each name
                        with no type of its own), [NO]UNUSED (names only
                        declared, and labels nothing refers to), and
                        [NO]ALIGNMENT (fields and common names off their natural
                        boundaries).  /WARNINGS alone is ALL and /NOWARNINGS is
                        NONE.  What isn't shown doesn't count: a compilation
                        with only those ends with a good status.

        /STANDARD[=(<keyword>,...)]  Flags what FORTRAN-77 doesn't have, each an
        /NOSTANDARD     informational message, so the program still compiles:
                        SYNTAX (statements, constants, operators, specifiers,
                        edit descriptors, names, intrinsic functions),
                        SOURCE_FORM (tabs, small letters, ! comments, debugging
                        lines, past column 72, over 19 continuation lines), ALL,
                        NONE, and NOSYNTAX and NOSOURCE_FORM.  /STANDARD alone
                        is ALL.  The default is /NOSTANDARD.

        /DEBUG[=(<keyword>,...)]  What MACRO is to write for the debugger: ALL,
        /NODEBUG        NONE, [NO]SYMBOLS (debugger records), and [NO]TRACEBACK
                        (a traceback's names).  /DEBUG alone is ALL, so a plain
                        MACRO then writes debugger records.  /NODEBUG, the
                        default, is traceback alone.  It doesn't turn the
                        optimizer off: give /NOOPTIMIZE as well to debug.

        /NOF77          FORTRAN-66's reading of what FORTRAN-77 changed: a DO runs
                        at least once, OPEN's defaults are STATUS='NEW' and
                        BLANK='ZERO', and EXTERNAL means a library function
                        of that name unless written *name.  The default is
                        /F77.  An OPTIONS statement before a unit may change it
                        for that unit.

        /RECURSIVE      Every FUNCTION and SUBROUTINE may be active more than
        /NORECURSIVE    once at a time, as if each said RECURSIVE: its local
                        variables are in its stack frame unless SAVE'd, given
                        initial values, in COMMON, or STATIC.  The default is
                        /NORECURSIVE.  An OPTIONS statement before a unit may
                        change it for that unit.

        /OBJECT[=<file>]  Runs MACRO on the MACRO file too, for an object file,
        /NOOBJECT       by default the source's name with type OBJ.  The MACRO
                        file is kept, so /OBJECT can't go with /NOMACRO.  The
                        default is /NOOBJECT.

    The compiler reports each problem it finds with the line it is on:

        %FORT-E-NOTIMPL, the DICTIONARY statement is not implemented yet
          at line 7 of DUA0:[WORK]PROG.FOR;1
                ENCODE (8, 10, BUF) X
          ------^

    After an error (E) or a fatal error (F) no MACRO file is written, so
    a failed compilation never replaces a good one, and the command ends
    with DCL-E-FORERRORS.  After warnings (W) the file is written and
    $STATUS is a warning.  With several sources, each failure is shown as
    it happens and the others are still compiled.

    From the host shell, "govax fortran <source>..." runs this command,
    with --macro <file> or --no-macro, --extend-source, --d-lines,
    --continuations <n>, --no-i4, --g-floating, --align <value>,
    --no-align, --optimize, --no-optimize, --list, --list-file <file>,
    --show <keywords>, --cross-reference, --check <keywords>,
    --warnings <keywords>, --standard <keywords>, --debug <keywords>,
    --object, --object-file <file>, --no-f77, and --recursive.

    Examples:

        FORTRAN SQUARES

        FORTRAN "squares.for"

        FORTRAN DUA0:[SRC]PROG /MACRO=DUA0:[OBJ]

        FORTRAN A,B,C

        FORTRAN DUNGEON /CONTINUATIONS=40

        FORTRAN PROG /ALIGN=(RECORDS=NATURAL,COMMONS=STANDARD)

        FORTRAN PROG /LIST /CROSS_REFERENCE /SHOW=INCLUDE

        FORTRAN PROG /CHECK=BOUNDS /WARNINGS=(DECLARATIONS,UNUSED)

        FORTRAN PROG /STANDARD=SYNTAX

        FORTRAN/DEBUG/NOOPTIMIZE/OBJECT PROG    ! then LINK/DEBUG PROG

        $ govax --mount-write DUA1=work.dsk fortran DUA1:[000000]PROG.FOR

    See also the help for MACRO, LINK, and RUN.
